 "ingredients": "150g hake · 100g rice vermicelli · 30g dill · 20g spring onion · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g peanuts · 15g limes · 15ml fish sauce · 10g galangal · 10g shallots · 8g turmeric · 5ml rice vinegar · 5g shrimp paste · 5g garlic · 5g chilli · 3g white sugar · salt",
 "method": "⚖️ THE DILL IS NOT A GARNISH. IT IS A VEGETABLE, AND YOU NEED FAR MORE OF IT THAN YOU THINK. Thirty grams for one person is a fistful — a bunch, not a sprig — and it goes into a hot pan in a heap that looks absurd for about four seconds before it collapses to nothing. ⛔ If you can count the fronds on the plate, you have used a tenth of what this dish needs. Every other instruction below is straightforward. This one is the dish.\n\n▶️ WHY A NORTHERN VIETNAMESE DISH IS BUILT ON A HERB YOU ASSOCIATE WITH SALMON. ⚖️ Dill reads as European — Scandinavian, Polish, a thing that happens to fish in cold countries — and that reading is geography rather than culture. Dill is a cool-season plant. Hanoi has a real winter, cool enough for months at a time to grow it in quantity, and no other kitchen in Southeast Asia does. ✅ So this is not a borrowed herb in an Asian dish. It is a cool-climate herb growing in the one corner of the region that gets cold, and the trivia on this card explains exactly where that line falls.\n\n▶️ MARINATE THE FISH IN TURMERIC AND GALANGAL, AND GIVE IT AT LEAST AN HOUR. Cut the hake into pieces about four centimetres square — chunky, not thin. Grate the galangal fine, mix it with the turmeric, the crushed garlic, the shrimp paste, the rice vinegar and a pinch of salt, and turn the fish through it. ⚖️ THE ACID IS DOING STRUCTURAL WORK, NOT FLAVOUR WORK. In Hanoi this is mẻ, a soured fermented rice, and it is there because a little acid firms the surface proteins of the fish just enough that the pieces hold together in the pan instead of flaking apart. ⚠️ Rice vinegar is the honest stand-in here and it works, but keep it to the amount listed — an hour in more acid than that and you are making ceviche, and the fish will go opaque and chalky before it ever meets heat.\n\n⚠️ TURMERIC STAINS EVERYTHING IT TOUCHES AND SOME OF IT PERMANENTLY. Wooden spoons, plastic chopping boards, grout, and the pads of your fingers for about two days. ✅ Use a glass or metal bowl, and if you have gloves, this is the recipe for them.\n\n▶️ THE SHRIMP PASTE IS FIVE GRAMS AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. ⚖️ At this quantity you will not taste it as shrimp paste — you will taste the fish as more intensely itself, because glutamates and nucleotides from a fermented seafood product amplify savoury depth in another seafood far beyond what the same weight of salt would do. ⛔ Leave it out and the dish is pleasant and slightly hollow, and you will not be able to name what is missing. ⚠️ Mash it into the marinade properly. A lump of it against one piece of fish is unpleasant in a way the spread-out version never is.\n\n▶️ COOK THE NOODLES, RINSE THEM COLD, AND DRAIN THEM PROPERLY BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE HAPPENS. Three minutes in boiling water, into a colander, cold tap until they are genuinely cold, then shake hard and leave to drain a full five minutes. ⚖️ They are served at room temperature under hot fish, and a wet noodle dilutes everything that lands on it.\n\n▶️ TOAST THE PEANUTS DRY AND CHOP THEM COARSE. Medium heat, moving constantly, until they smell roasted, then straight out onto a cold plate. ⚠️ They carry on cooking in a hot pan off the heat and go bitter within about thirty seconds of looking perfect.\n\n▶️ FRY THE FISH HARD, IN A HOT PAN, AND DO NOT MOVE IT. Get the oil properly hot — shimmering, not smoking — lay the pieces in with space between them, and leave them alone for three minutes. ✅ You are after a set golden crust on the first face before you turn anything. ⚠️ Fish sticks to a pan that is not hot enough, and it tears when you try to rescue it, because there is nothing holding it together yet; fish lets go of the pan by itself once the crust has set, which is why waiting works and levering does not. If it does not lift cleanly, it is not ready, and the fix is to wait rather than to lever.\n\n▶️ NOW THE HAYSTACK, AND THE WHOLE THING TAKES SIXTY SECONDS. Turn the fish, drop the heat to medium, and pile the entire quantity of dill and the spring onion cut into long batons straight on top. ✅ Give it a minute, turning the herbs through the hot oil rather than through the fish, until the dill has just gone limp and darkened and still smells green. ⛔ THE MOMENT IT GOES OLIVE AND SMELLS COOKED, IT IS OVER. Dill's aroma compounds are volatile, which is why they leave in a single puff of steam — so this is a wilt, not a cook, and the difference between the two is about ninety seconds.\n\n▶️ SERVE IT STRAIGHT FROM THE PAN, AND BUILD EACH BOWL AT THE TABLE. Cold noodles in the bowl, hot fish and herbs spooned over, peanuts scattered, and the nước chấm made from the fish sauce, lime juice, sugar, chopped chilli and about 40ml of warm water poured over at the last second. ⚖️ In Hanoi the pan arrives on a small burner still spitting and everyone assembles their own, and that is not theatre. The herbs keep going soft from the moment they leave the heat, so that a bowl built in the kitchen arrives three minutes older than it should be.",
