 "trivia": "The herb map of Southeast Asia is drawn by temperature, not by borders, and this dish sits exactly on one of its lines. Dill is a cool-season umbellifer from the eastern Mediterranean and western Asia — it germinates and grows well in mild weather and it bolts, thins and turns bitter under sustained tropical heat, which is why it is essentially absent from Thai, Malay, Indonesian and southern Vietnamese cooking. Those kitchens run on herbs built for the heat instead: Thai basil, sawtooth coriander, Vietnamese mint, all of them tolerant of conditions that would finish dill in a fortnight. Northern Vietnam is the exception, and it is an exception of latitude. The Red River Delta sits far enough north to get a genuine cool season, with months where the daily average sits in the mid-teens, and dill grows there in quantity through exactly that window. So the usual reading of this dish — a European herb turning up unexpectedly in an Asian pan, presumably via the French — has the causation backwards. Nothing was borrowed. A cool-climate plant is being used in the one part of Southeast Asia with a cool climate, by cooks who have had it locally and abundantly for as long as anyone has been cooking there. The quiet consequence is that chả cá is seasonal in a way no menu ever mentions: in Hanoi it belongs to the cold months, because that is when there is enough dill to bury a fish in.",
