 "occasion": ["everyday", "family", "winter", "quick", "lunch"],
 "ingredients": "250g chicken pieces · 40g ginger · 20g shallots · 20ml fish sauce · 15g white sugar · 10ml sunflower oil · 10g spring onion · 10g garlic · 5g chilli · black pepper · salt",
 "method": "⚖️ THE GINGER GOES IN TWICE, AND THE TWO HALVES ARE DOING OPPOSITE JOBS. Half at the start, half in the last two minutes. ✅ This is the whole card, it costs nothing, and almost nobody does it — because the reason only makes sense once you know that ginger's heat is not one compound but three, and that a wet braise systematically converts the sharp one into the sweet one. The trivia below sets that out properly. ⛔ Put all the ginger in at the start and you get a pot that is round, warm and slightly flat. Put it all in at the end and you get raw heat sitting on top of a sauce it never joined.\n\n▶️ BUY OLD GINGER, NOT YOUNG, AND KNOW WHICH IS WHICH. Old ginger has tight brown skin, visible fibres at a cut face, and a hard snap. Young ginger is pale, thin-skinned, faintly pink at the tips and snaps wet. ⚖️ Vietnamese cooks specify gừng già — old — for a kho pot, because it carries more of the pungent compounds and stands up to twenty-five minutes of simmering. ⚠️ Young ginger is lovely and it will disappear in this pot without leaving anything behind.\n\n▶️ CUT IT INTO MATCHSTICKS, NOT GRATED, AND CUT THEM WITH THE GRAIN. Slice the piece lengthways into thin planks, stack them, and cut down into fine sticks. ⚖️ Grated ginger dissolves and the pot has no ginger IN it, only ginger flavour — and this dish is one you should be able to pick a piece of ginger out of and eat on purpose. ✅ Cutting along the fibre rather than across it gives sticks that hold together instead of falling into threads.\n\n▶️ MAKE THE CARAMEL, AND TREAT IT AS PLUMBING RATHER THAN AS THE POINT. Sugar and a tablespoon of water in the pot over medium heat, no stirring, until it goes the colour of dark honey and starts to smell nutty. ⚠️ It moves from amber to black in about fifteen seconds at the end, so stand there. ✅ The instant it is right, take it off the heat and add the fish sauce at arm's length — it will spit violently and seize, and it will loosen again the moment the heat comes back on. ⚖️ This is nước màu, standard machinery across the whole kho family, and Bò Kho's card carries the family's story. Here it is doing colour and a bitter edge against the sweetness, and nothing more.\n\n▶️ BROWN THE CHICKEN IN THE CARAMEL, SKIN DOWN, AND GIVE IT TIME. Oil in, chicken pieces skin-side down into the loosened caramel, and four or five minutes without moving them. ✅ You want the skin properly coloured and some of the fat rendered out before any liquid arrives. ⛔ Chicken added to a pot that already has liquid in it will never brown at any point afterwards, and the dish will taste boiled no matter how long you give it.\n\n▶️ NOW HALF THE GINGER, THE SHALLOTS AND THE GARLIC, THEN ENOUGH WATER TO COME HALFWAY UP. Not to cover. ⚖️ A kho pot is a shallow braise that reduces down onto the meat, and drowning it means either a thin sauce at the end or forty minutes of boiling it back. ✅ Halfway up the chicken, lid off, and a steady but gentle bubble.\n\n⏱️ TWENTY-FIVE MINUTES, AND WATCH THE LIQUID RATHER THAN THE CLOCK. It is ready when the sauce has reduced to a glossy layer that coats the back of a spoon and the chicken is coming away from the bone. ⚠️ If it reduces faster than the chicken cooks, add a splash of water; if the chicken is done and the sauce is thin, lift the chicken out and boil the sauce hard for two minutes on its own. ⛔ Never reduce a sauce with meat still sitting in it past the point the meat needed — that is how a good braise becomes a dry one.\n\n▶️ THE SECOND HALF OF THE GINGER GOES IN WITH TWO MINUTES LEFT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK. Stir it through, put the lid on, take the pot off the heat. ✅ Two minutes is enough for the sticks to soften slightly and lose their raw edge, and not nearly enough for the braise to sweeten them the way it sweetened the first half. ⚖️ You now have two different gingers in one sauce, and the difference is genuinely audible on the tongue — a warm sweetness underneath and a bright sting on top.\n\n▶️ BLACK PEPPER OFF THE HEAT, AND MORE OF IT THAN FEELS RIGHT. A heavy grind, stirred through at the very end with the sliced spring onion. ⚖️ Vietnamese kho dishes are peppery in a way the ingredient list never quite prepares you for, and pepper added at the start of a braise loses most of its aroma to twenty-five minutes of steam. ✅ At the end it stays sharp and sits alongside the late ginger rather than under it.",
