 "storage": "Better on day two and keeps 3 days in the fridge, where the sauce sets to a soft jelly and loosens the moment it is warmed. Freezes 2 months. ⚠️ Reheat gently and add a few fresh ginger matchsticks at the end — the late-ginger sting is the first thing to go overnight, and thirty seconds of work puts it straight back.",
 "chefNotes": "⚖️ Split the ginger. That one instruction costs nothing and adds no time, and it is what separates a pleasant braise from one people ask you about afterwards.\n\n✅ Old ginger, cut into matchsticks with the grain. Grated ginger vanishes and this dish wants pieces you can find.\n\n⚠️ The caramel goes from right to ruined in about fifteen seconds. Stay at the pot and have the fish sauce open and ready before you start it.\n\n⛔ Never cover the chicken with liquid. Halfway up is the instruction and it is not a suggestion — a kho reduces onto the meat, it does not poach it.",
 "pairsWith": "Canh Chua alongside · Xôi Xéo or plain rice · Chè Chuối after",
 "goesWith": "A bowl of plain rice · Sliced cucumber · Extra chilli in fish sauce",
 "trivia": "Ginger is one of the few ingredients where the cooking method does not merely change how much heat you taste but which heat compound you are tasting at all. Raw ginger's pungency comes mainly from gingerol, and heat sends it two separate ways. Drying converts gingerol to shogaol, which is substantially more pungent than the compound it came from — which is why dried ground ginger is hotter than the fresh root it was made from. But a second reaction runs alongside it: with plenty of water at moderate heat, gingerol cleaves instead into zingerone, which is mild, faintly sweet and smells warm rather than sharp. Which reaction dominates depends almost entirely on how wet the pot is. A dry roast or a drying rack drives toward shogaol and more heat. A long wet braise drives toward zingerone and less. A kho pot is about as wet and as moderate as cooking gets, which makes it a highly efficient machine for turning sharp ginger into sweet ginger — and explains why a braise built on a large amount of ginger can somehow finish tasting hardly gingery at all. The split addition is a cook taking both outcomes on purpose instead of accepting one. The first half spends twenty-five minutes becoming zingerone and dissolving into the sauce as warmth; the second gets two minutes, enough to lose its raw edge and nowhere near enough to convert, so it reaches the table still carrying its gingerol. Two chemically different ingredients from one root, in one pot.",
