   "howThisFeels": "The same soft dished cake, with something that crackles landing in the middle of it.",
   "trivia": "Tóp mỡ is pork fat rendered until the solids left behind go crisp, and across central Vietnam it turns up scattered on almost anything soft. ⚖️ It earns its place here on texture rather than richness — a bánh bèo is soft cake, soft floss and liquid sauce, with nothing in the mouthful that resists the teeth, and the fried shallot alone is too light to fix that. ⚠️ Render the fat slowly over low heat and lift the solids out the moment they turn gold; rushed over high heat they go hard and taste scorched rather than sweet.",
