 "ingredients": "200g tofu · 200g tomatoes · 40g onion · 25ml sunflower oil · 20g tomato paste · 20ml soy sauce · 15g spring onion · 10g garlic · 8g white sugar · 5g chilli · black pepper · salt",
 "method": "⚖️ EVERY BRAISE YOU HAVE EVER COOKED SEASONS THE OUTSIDE OF THINGS. Meat is already full — its cells are saturated with their own water, so a sauce gets a few millimetres in and stops, which is why beef cubes get cut small and why a stew is seasoned to be eaten with something plain. ✅ Tofu is the one common protein where that is not true. It arrives with genuine empty space inside it, and pressing makes more. So this is not a dish where you put sauce on tofu. It is a dish where you make room and then fill it, and the two instructions below that nobody bothers with are the ones that decide whether it works.\n\n▶️ FIRST INSTRUCTION NOBODY FOLLOWS: WEIGH THE BLOCK BEFORE AND AFTER YOU PRESS IT. Thirty minutes under something genuinely heavy — a pan with tins in it, not a side plate. ⚖️ A firm block should come out having lost somewhere around a sixth of its weight, and that lost weight is exactly the volume of sauce it can take back. ⛔ If it has shed almost nothing, it was either not firm tofu or the weight was too light, and no amount of simmering later will fix it, because there is nowhere for the liquid to go. ✅ Thirty seconds on a kitchen scale tells you now what you would otherwise find out at the table.\n\n▶️ CUT IT INTO THICK SLABS, NOT CUBES. About a centimetre and a half, and cut them after pressing rather than before. ⚖️ Cubes give you more surface and less inside, and the inside is the part doing the work here. ⚠️ Cutting before pressing means the pieces squash out of shape and the fried faces come out uneven.\n\n▶️ FRY IT PROPERLY GOLDEN ON TWO FACES, IN MORE OIL THAN FEELS NECESSARY. Hot pan, the oil shimmering, slabs laid in with space, four minutes a face without moving them. ✅ You want a real skin, deep gold and slightly leathery, because that skin is what holds the slab together during the braise while the middle softens. ⛔ Pale tofu falls apart in the sauce, and a crowded pan steams instead of frying.\n\n▶️ SECOND INSTRUCTION NOBODY FOLLOWS, AND IT IS THE WHOLE TRICK: LET THE FRIED TOFU COOL BEFORE IT MEETS THE SAUCE. Onto a rack, five minutes, while you make the tomato. ⚖️ A block straight out of the pan is full of steam, and steam is taking up every bit of the space you just spent thirty minutes creating — so sauce arriving at that moment finds the pores occupied and pressurised, wets the surface and gets no further. ✅ As it cools the steam condenses back to almost nothing, the pressure inside drops below the pressure outside, and the block draws liquid in on its own. ⛔ This is genuinely the opposite of what everyone does, and it is the difference between tomato-coated tofu and tomato-filled tofu.\n\n▶️ NOW THE SAUCE, AND KEEP IT THIN. ⚖️ Everything about a good Italian tomato sauce — long, reduced, concentrated, clinging — is wrong here, because a thick sauce cannot be absorbed by anything and will simply sit on top. ✅ Onion in the same pan for three minutes until soft, garlic for thirty seconds, then the tomato paste for one minute until it darkens toward brick, then the fresh tomatoes chopped roughly with about 100ml of water. Eight minutes at a brisk simmer, no longer.\n\n▶️ BREAK THE TOMATOES DOWN BUT DO NOT COOK THEM TO SAUCE. ✅ You are after a loose, bright, slightly chunky liquid — the pieces should still be recognisable and the whole thing should pour rather than drop off a spoon. ⚠️ Push it past that and it goes sweet, dark and jammy, which is a perfectly nice thing and a different dish. Vietnamese sốt cà is sharp and fresh and thin on purpose.\n\n▶️ SEASON THE SAUCE, THEN SLIDE THE COOLED TOFU IN AND STOP STIRRING. Soy sauce, sugar, a good grind of black pepper, salt to taste. Tofu in, spooned over once, lid on, and eight minutes at the barest simmer. ⛔ Do not stir it again. The skin is holding each slab together and a spoon dragged through the pan is the only thing that will break them.\n\n▶️ TAKE IT OFF THE HEAT AND GIVE IT TEN MINUTES BEFORE ANYONE EATS. ⚖️ Same reason as the cooling step, running one more time: the tofu keeps drinking as the pan comes down in temperature, so a dish served the second the timer goes has done maybe half its absorbing. ✅ Spring onion over the top at the end, and it is genuinely better lukewarm than scalding.",
