 "trivia": "The gap between what a Vietnamese family eats and what a Vietnamese restaurant serves is wider than for most cuisines, and this dish sits squarely on the family side of it. Almost nothing that made the country's food famous abroad is weeknight cooking at home. Phở is bought, not made — three hours of bones is a shop's job, not a Tuesday's. Bánh mì comes from a cart. Bún chả means going out. What actually appears on a home table most nights is a completely different and much quieter repertoire built on the same handful of things: rice, a bowl of canh, something green, and one modest dish of protein in a sauce, of which tofu in tomato is close to the most common in the whole country. It is the Vietnamese equivalent of the meals no cuisine bothers to export — the ones that never got a restaurant, a photograph or a reputation, and that everybody actually grew up eating. What is quietly interesting about that is how much technique hides inside something this ordinary. The dish is doing something no meat braise can do at all: it is being seasoned from the inside rather than the outside, using a protein that arrives with space in it and a cook who knows to make more. A stew is a compromise with the fact that meat is already full. This is not, and that is why an eight-minute sauce and a block of the cheapest protein in the shop can end up tasting of more than a dish that simmered for two hours.",
 "howThisFeels": "A cheap pan of red and gold on a Tuesday, tasting of considerably more than it cost.",
