 "ingredients": "150g avocado · 45g condensed milk · 50ml milk · 10g limes · salt",
 "method": "⚖️ FREEZE THE AVOCADO, NOT THE WATER. This is the one instruction that separates a good glass from the thin, faintly grassy thing most people make, and it takes no skill at all — cube the flesh, spread it on a plate so the pieces are not touching, and freeze it for at least two hours. ✅ Ice cubes do exactly two things in a blender: they make it cold, and then they melt and dilute everything you put in there. Frozen avocado does the first and never does the second, so the glass tastes the same at the last mouthful as it did at the first.\n\n▶️ THE THICKNESS IS COMING FROM FAT, NOT FROM ICE, AND THAT CHANGES HOW YOU BLEND. Avocado is roughly a fifth fat by weight and it has almost no fibrous structure left once it is broken down, which is why it can give a drink the body of soft-serve with no cream, no thickener and no dairy needed for texture at all. ⚖️ But fat behaves according to its temperature. Avocado fat is largely oleic acid, which is soft and spreadable when cold and fully liquid when warm — so a blender running for two minutes heats the mixture by friction, the fat thins out, and the drink goes from thick to loose and can leave an oily film at the top of the glass. ⛔ Blend in short bursts. Twenty seconds, stop, scrape down, twenty more. Stop the moment it is smooth.\n\n▶️ PICK THE AVOCADO PROPERLY, BECAUSE NOTHING BELOW RESCUES A BAD ONE. Yielding to gentle pressure in the palm, not the fingertips, and no soft spots or dents. ⚠️ Flick the small stem nub off the top: green underneath means ready, brown means it has gone past and there will be stringy dark fibres through the flesh that a blender turns into grey specks rather than removing. ✅ Firm and unripe is the easier fault to fix — give it three days on the counter and try again.\n\n▶️ CONDENSED MILK RATHER THAN SUGAR AND MILK, AND IT IS DOING TWO JOBS. It sweetens, obviously. ⚖️ Less obviously it is bringing dissolved milk solids and a great deal of viscosity of its own, so the drink gets its sweetness and part of its body from the same spoon — sweetening with sugar and thinning with milk gives you the same sugar and a noticeably looser glass. ⛔ Evaporated milk is not a substitute. It is unsweetened, and using it gives you something pale, thin and slightly savoury.\n\n▶️ A PINCH OF SALT AND A SQUEEZE OF LIME, AND BOTH ARE STRUCTURAL. Avocado is fatty and mild and condensed milk is sweet and mild, so a glass with neither reads as flat and slightly cloying by the third mouthful. ✅ Salt sharpens the sweetness rather than adding to it, and the lime gives the fat something to push against. ⚠️ Lime juice also slows the browning — cut avocado darkens because an enzyme in the flesh reacts with oxygen, and acid slows that enzyme down considerably, which is why a lime-squeezed glass still looks green half an hour later.\n\n▶️ BLEND IN THE RIGHT ORDER: LIQUIDS FIRST, FROZEN LAST. Milk, condensed milk, lime juice and salt into the jug, then the frozen avocado on top. ⚖️ A blender blade needs liquid around it to create the vortex that pulls solids down; frozen chunks at the bottom sit under the blade in a dead spot and the machine screams at nothing. ✅ If yours stalls anyway, stop it and stir rather than adding water — water is the one thing this drink does not want.\n\n▶️ TASTE IT BEFORE IT GOES IN THE GLASS AND EXPECT TO ADJUST. Avocados vary enormously in fat and in how much flavour they carry, so a fixed quantity of condensed milk is a starting point rather than a rule. ✅ It should be sweet, faintly salty, and thick enough that a spoon stands up in it for a second before it falls. ⚠️ Too thick is a two-second fix with a splash more milk. Too thin cannot be fixed at all once the avocado is in there, which is why you start at the thick end.\n\n▶️ SERVE IT IMMEDIATELY, IN A COLD GLASS, WITH A SPOON AS WELL AS A STRAW. ⚖️ Twenty minutes on a counter and the fat warms, the body goes and it separates. ✅ Chill the glass while you blend. And a straw alone is optimistic — done properly this is closer to a dessert you eat than a drink you finish.",
