 "goesWith": "A spoon as well as a straw · Toasted sesame seeds on top · A second glass, realistically",
 "trivia": "Whether a fruit belongs in the sweet drawer or the savoury one is decided almost entirely by what was already on the table when it arrived, and the avocado is the clearest case going. Vietnam grows most of its avocados around Đà Lạt in the central highlands, where French planters put them in the 1940s because the altitude offered a climate the lowlands could not. What was already on the Vietnamese table was an entire established category of rich, sweet, spoonable things — chè, coconut milk, condensed milk, sweetened bean pastes — and a fatty, mild, faintly sweet fruit walked straight into it without anyone having to think about it. The same fruit reached the anglophone world along a completely different road. It was sold in the United States for decades as the alligator pear, a name the growers eventually decided was costing them sales, and the industry that renamed it then spent most of a century positioning it as a salad and sandwich item — partly because that is where the money was, and partly because the American table had no fatty-sweet shelf for it to join. Neither placement has much to do with the fruit. It is not sweeter in Đà Lạt and not more savoury in California; it is mild and fatty in both, which is exactly why it needed a culture to decide for it. Brazil, Indonesia and the Philippines all sweeten it too, and all three had the same shelf already open when it arrived.",
 "howThisFeels": "Pale green, thicker than a drink has any right to be, and gone considerably faster than you meant.",
